{"text": "Concer nv and Meta: Ble and\n\u2018herpes Aces ete by Garth\n\u2018Neston and ka Ms. Raven Pros,\nNew You @ 1504\n\nRole of Prostacyclin as a Natural Deterrent to\nHematogenous Tumor Metastasis\n\nKenneth V. Honn,*++ David G. Menter,t James M. Onoda,*\nJohn D. Tayior,* and Bonnie F. Sloane*\u00a7\n\nDepartments of * Radiation Oncology, + Radiology. \u00a2 Biological Sciences, and\n{\u00a7 Pharmacology, Wayne State University, Detrol, Michigan 48202\n\nA metastatic lesion represents the result of a series of sequential events in\nwhich neoplastic cells are released from a primary tumor and disseminate to\nsecondary sites. This process has been termed the \u201cmetastatic cascade\u201d (Figure\n1). The primary tumor is believed tobe initiated by transformation (.e, chemical,\nradiation, or viral) of @ normal cell, which then proliferates until limited by\nthe blood supply and invasive properties of the tumor. As the tumor grows,\nthe tumor eels release angiogenesis factors, which induce neovascularization.\n\u2018To move to a secondary site, the tumor cells must be able to detach from the\nprimary tumor and invade into the circulatory system (intravasate), where the\ntumor cells may interact with host platelets and neutrophils during transport.\n\u2018Tumor cells can induce platelet aggregation, and tur is thought to\nfacilitate attachment of tumor cells to the endothelium or to areas of exposed\n\u2018basement membrane. Dissolution of the basement membrane by proteolytic en-\nzymes released from the tumor cell then may enable the cells to extravasate\nat a scondary site, where the processes of proliferation, angiogenesis, invasion,\nand metastasis (from a metastasis) can reoceur.\nMetastasis is an inefficient process, since only 0.001% of cells that detach\nfrom the primary tumor are estimated to survive and form a metastasic lesion\n(Weiss 1980, 1982, 1983). Survival at each step in the metastatic cascade can\nbbe altered by pathophysiological properties of the tumor cells (Le, \u201cpre-existing\n\u2018metastatic phenotype\"; Poste and Fidler 1980) or by properties of the host\n(.e,, tumor cell/platelet interaction; Honn et al. 19832). Survival of 0.001%\nof the tumor eells entering the metastatic cascade might also be explained exclu-\nsively in terms of random events (Weiss 1983). It is likely that random and\nnonrandom events act in concert.\n\u2018The presence of viable circulating tumor cells does not necessarily indicate\npoor prognosis (Salsbury 1975). Successful arrest or adhesion of the tumor\ncells to the vessel wall and the factors (eg., platelets, endothelial cell surface\ndeterminants, tumor cell surface determinants, ete.) that activate or enhance\nthis process may be the most important events in metastasis. Carcinomas were\n\n361\n\n50254599",
